---
import BaseLayout from "theme/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const lang = "en";
const dir = "ltr";
const slug = "first-day-of-fall-2026";
const seoTitle = "First Day of Fall 2026 | September Equinox Date & Time";
const pageDescription = "The first day of fall 2026 is September 22 in the US. See the exact September equinox time and what the equinox means.";

const pubDate = "2026-09-20T10:00:00+05:00";
const updatedDate = "2026-09-24T12:00:00+05:00";

const meta = {
  seoTitle,
  exactTitle: true,
  description: pageDescription,
  keywords: "first day of fall 2026, autumn equinox 2026, september equinox 2026 date and time, when does fall start 2026, astronomical fall",
  pubDate,
  updatedDate,
  author: "Time Intelligence Team",
  faqs: [
    {
      q: "When does fall start in 2026?",
      a: "Astronomical fall started on Tuesday, September 22, 2026, in the United States, at the September equinox."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the September equinox?",
      a: "It is the moment the Sun crosses the celestial equator heading south, marking the start of astronomical autumn in the Northern Hemisphere and spring in the Southern Hemisphere."
    },
    {
      q: "Are day and night exactly equal on the equinox?",
      a: "No, not exactly. Day is usually a few minutes longer because the Sun is a disc and the atmosphere bends its light."
    },
    {
      q: "Is the first day of fall the same as the autumn equinox?",
      a: "Yes, for astronomical fall. Meteorological fall, used for weather records, starts on September 1."
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout
  lang={lang}
  dir={dir}
  title={seoTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  slug={slug}
  meta={meta}
  enableFaqSchema={true}
  enableArticleSchema={true}
  allowAds={true}
>
  <!-- Hero Banner -->
  <div class="hero-banner">
    <h1 class="hero-banner-title">First Day of Fall 2026</h1>
    <p style="color: #bdd2dc; font-size: clamp(1rem, 2.2vw, 1.25rem); max-width: 640px; margin: 12px auto 0;">
      The astronomical arrival of autumn and the exact timing of the September equinox.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto; padding-top: 24px;">

    <!-- Quick Answer Block Directly Below H1 (Server-Rendered) -->
    <div style="background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 12%, var(--panel)); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 28px; box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);">
      <span style="display: block; font-size: 0.8rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--accent-2); font-weight: 800; margin-bottom: 6px;">Quick Answer</span>
      <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 1.12rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--text); font-weight: 600;">
        The first day of fall 2026 in the Northern Hemisphere was Tuesday, September 22, 2026. The September equinox occurred at 00:05 UTC on Wednesday, September 23, 2026, which was 8:05 p.m. EDT on September 22 in the eastern United States. The same moment marks the first day of spring in the Southern Hemisphere.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Hero Image -->
    <div class="infographic-wrapper">
      <img
        src="/assets/first-day-of-fall-2026-hero.webp"
        alt="Autumn leaves in warm colours marking the first day of fall and the September equinox 2026"
        class="infographic-image"
        width="960"
        height="540"
        loading="eager"
      />
    </div>

    <!-- H2: When is the first day of fall 2026? -->
    <section>
      <h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 2rem); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); padding-bottom: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">When is the first day of fall 2026?</h2>
      <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 72ch;">
        The first day of fall 2026 arrived at the precise astronomical instant of the September equinox: <strong>00:05 UTC on Wednesday, September 23, 2026</strong>. In the eastern United States, that corresponding moment was <strong>8:05 p.m. EDT on Tuesday, September 22, 2026</strong>.
      </p>
      <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 72ch; margin-top: 12px;">
        Because the equinox occurs at a single simultaneous moment across the globe, the calendar date on which it falls depends strictly on your local time zone. In the Americas (including Pacific, Mountain, Central, and Eastern time zones), the moment took place before midnight on September 22. In UTC and regions further east (including Europe, Africa, Asia, and Australia), local clocks had already crossed into September 23.
      </p>
      <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 72ch; margin-top: 12px;">
        The equinox does not fall on September 22 every single year. Depending on leap year cycles and orbital mechanics, the September equinox can occur on September 22 or September 23, and very occasionally on September 21 or 24.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Diagram -->
    <div class="infographic-wrapper">
      <figure style="margin: 0; text-align: center;">
        <img
          src="/assets/september-equinox-explained.webp"
          alt="Diagram showing the Sun over the equator at the September equinox"
          class="infographic-image"
          width="800"
          height="450"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="infographic-caption">At the equinox, neither hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: What is the autumn equinox? -->
    <section>
      <h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 2rem); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); padding-bottom: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">What is the autumn equinox?</h2>
      <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 72ch;">
        The word <em>equinox</em> comes from Latin meaning "equal night." At this astronomical position in Earth's orbit, the Sun crosses the celestial equator heading southward, meaning neither the Northern Hemisphere nor the Southern Hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun.
      </p>
      <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 72ch; margin-top: 12px;">
        While it is commonly taught that day and night are of identical length on the equinox, daylight is actually slightly longer than 12 hours. Two physical realities cause this difference: first, the Sun is an optical disc rather than a geometric point, so daytime begins the second its top edge crests the horizon and only ends when its final edge disappears. Second, atmospheric refraction bends solar rays upward, allowing us to see sunlight minutes before actual geometric sunrise and after geometric sunset.
      </p>
      <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 72ch; margin-top: 12px;">
        The date when day and night are truly equal in duration is called the <em>equilux</em>. Unlike the equinox, which happens at the exact same instant worldwide, the equilux occurs several days after the autumn equinox and varies depending on a location's exact latitude.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- H2: Astronomical fall vs meteorological fall -->
    <section>
      <h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 2rem); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); padding-bottom: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">Astronomical fall vs meteorological fall</h2>
      <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 72ch;">
        Scientists and climatologists recognize two definitions of autumn, each serving a valid and distinct purpose:
      </p>
      <ul style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; padding-left: 20px; margin: 12px 0;">
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text);">Astronomical Fall:</strong> Defined strictly by celestial mechanics and Earth's axial position relative to the Sun. It begins at the September equinox and concludes at the December solstice.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text);">Meteorological Fall:</strong> Defined by annual temperature cycles and whole calendar months. Meteorologists group September 1 through November 30 as autumn to standardize seasonal weather statistics and record-keeping across years.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 72ch;">
        Both classification systems are scientifically correct within their respective domains.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- H2: What changes after the equinox? -->
    <section>
      <h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 2rem); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); padding-bottom: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">What changes after the equinox?</h2>
      <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 72ch;">
        In the Northern Hemisphere, days continue to grow progressively shorter and nights longer until the winter solstice in late December. Conversely, in the Southern Hemisphere, where the equinox heralds the arrival of spring, daylight hours expand and temperatures rise as the region tilts closer toward the Sun.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Internal Links -->
    <section>
      <div class="info-callout">
        <p>
          Following the autumn equinox, check <a href="/when-do-clocks-go-back-2026/" style="color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px;">When Do Clocks Go Back 2026</a> to prepare for the end of Daylight Saving Time. Keep track of current hours with the <a href="/full-screen-clock/" style="color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px;">Full Screen Clock</a>, or visit our <a href="/" style="color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px;">GMT to EST Converter</a> homepage.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <section style="max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 48px 0 24px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 2rem); text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      {meta.faqs.map(faq => (
        <details style="padding: 16px; background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; cursor: pointer;">{faq.q}</summary>
          <p style="margin: 12px 0 0; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.65;">{faq.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
